/// Top-level OTP supervision tree for the Exdura durable workflow engine.
///
/// Five components are supervised children in dependency order:
///   1. EventStore — durable event persistence
///   2. ActivityWorkerPool — supervised activity execution
///   3. WorkflowEngine — workflow orchestration (resolves deps by name)
///   4. TimerManager — durable timer scheduling (resolves deps by name)
///   5. ExduraHttpServerSupervisor — optional HTTP client API (SPEC.md §7.2)
///
/// Uses rest_for_one: if EventStore crashes, everything after it restarts.
/// If WorkflowEngine crashes, TimerManager and the HTTP supervisor restart
/// too. `ExduraHttpServerSupervisor` is always present (childless is the
/// normal, cheap default); `ExduraWorker start:` adds the actual
/// `ExduraHttpServer` child via `startChild:` when `#http` config is given.
/// A cascade restart triggered by an EventStore/WorkflowEngine crash tears
/// down that dynamically-added child along with it, and nothing currently
/// re-adds it — a known, still-open limitation.
///
/// All four core components are registered under fixed names (ADR 0079)
/// and resolve each other by name at use-time rather than caching pids —
/// see `WorkflowEngine>>eventStore`/`activityPool` and
/// `TimerManager>>engine`/`eventStore`. This is deliberate: an earlier
/// version wired dependencies once via a `class initialize: sup` hook,
/// documented as "re-runs on restart (rest_for_one)" — that claim was
/// wrong. `initialize:` fires exactly once, at the initial `supervise`
/// call; OTP-driven restarts start each child directly and never call it
/// again. A restarted `WorkflowEngine` came back with a nil `eventStore`
/// and crashed on the next workflow call. Named lookup sidesteps the
/// problem entirely — nothing needs to notice a restart happened.
///
///   beamtalk run                          // starts via beamtalk.toml
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// sup := ExduraSupervisor supervise     // or start manually
/// ```
typed Supervisor subclass: ExduraSupervisor

  /// rest_for_one: if a child crashes, all children after it restart.
  class strategy -> Symbol => #restForOne

  /// Child specs in dependency order.
  ///
  /// `ExduraHttpServerSupervisor` is a nested `DynamicSupervisor`, not an
  /// `Actor` — it's listed as a bare class reference rather than built via
  /// `supervisionSpec withRestart:` (which only `Actor` subclasses
  /// support). Nested supervisors default to `#permanent` restart.
  ///
  /// The other four register under fixed names (ADR 0079) so siblings —
  /// and external consumers like `ExduraClient`/`ExduraHttpServer` — can
  /// resolve them fresh after a restart instead of holding a pid captured
  /// at construction. `WorkflowEngine` additionally gets `#supervised =>
  /// true` in its args: unlike EventStore, a nil `activityPool` field is
  /// also a legitimate standalone state (Phase 0, no-pool mode via
  /// `WorkflowEngine withStore:`), so it needs an explicit signal to know
  /// "nil means look it up by name" applies here.
  ///
  /// Only one `ExduraSupervisor` may be alive at a time per node as a
  /// result of the fixed names; tests that spawn one must run serially
  /// (`class serial -> Boolean => true`).
  class children -> List =>
    storeSpec := EventStore supervisionSpec withName: #eventStore withRestart: #permanent
    poolSpec := ActivityWorkerPool supervisionSpec withName: #activityWorkerPool withRestart: #permanent
    engineSpec := (WorkflowEngine supervisionSpec)
      withName: #workflowEngine
      withRestart: #permanent
      withArgs: #{#supervised => true}
    timerSpec := TimerManager supervisionSpec withName: #timerManager withRestart: #permanent

    #(storeSpec, poolSpec, engineSpec, timerSpec, ExduraHttpServerSupervisor)
